<section class="page-hero"><span class="eyebrow">MIGRATION PLAYBOOK</span><h1>Change the platform.<br><em>Keep control of the move.</em></h1><p class="lede">Migration is a service-model translation, not just a configuration import. Start with a small cohort, explicit success criteria and a return path you have rehearsed.</p></section>
<section class="section"><span class="eyebrow">BEFORE THE WINDOW</span><h2>Map what subscribers actually use.</h2><div class="cards"><article><h3>Service inventory</h3><p>Record access identity, circuit keys, VLANs, RADIUS attributes, speeds, suspension rules, IPv4 modes and delegated prefixes. Identify exceptions before building the happy path.</p></article><article><h3>Address & route ownership</h3><p>Map pools, static reservations, announcements and return routes. Decide when the source stops owning a resource and the target may begin.</p></article><article><h3>Accounting & integrations</h3><p>Capture billing triggers, session identifiers, interim intervals and reconciliation rules. Recognise duplicates, missing records and retried requests.</p></article><article><h3>Unsupported settings</h3><p>Keep a gap list with an owner and decision: map, redesign, defer or block. Accepting a configuration does not prove equivalent service behaviour.</p></article></div></section>
<section class="section"><span class="eyebrow">A CONTROLLED TRANSITION</span><h2>Four gates to cutover.</h2><ol class="steps"><li><b>Reproduce</b><p>Build representative lab journeys: static IPv4, CGNAT, IPv6 and a suspended account.</p></li><li><b>Validate</b><p>Check bidirectional traffic, shaping, accounting, MTU, reconnection and dependency failures.</p></li><li><b>Move a cohort</b><p>Use a bounded group and agreed window. Watch experience, admission, pressure, logs and accounting.</p></li><li><b>Expand deliberately</b><p>Review evidence before increasing scope. Keep source service and recovery instructions available.</p></li></ol></section>
<section class="split section"><div><span class="eyebrow">ROLLBACK IS A WORKFLOW</span><h2>Know what must<br>move back.</h2><p>A return path needs the same care as the forward move. Agree thresholds and a decision owner before the window starts.</p><a class="button" href="contact.php?topic=Migration">Discuss your migration ↗</a></div><div class="stack"><article><h3>Traffic & ownership</h3><p>Restore source routes and remove conflicting target ownership in the agreed order. Guard against two systems assigning the same service.</p></article><article><h3>Subscriber state</h3><p>Define reconnection, old NAT mapping visibility and static-address restoration. Do not assume flows survive rollback.</p></article><article><h3>Commercial records</h3><p>Reconcile accounting and billing events generated during the window. Retain evidence that explains what happened.</p></article></div></section>
